(If you don't mind not singing along with the original - I mean, you can, but have to capo the 7th fret - there is also a version in C which is easier to play since it doesn't have the jump from (G) to (Bm) to (Em).)

TIP:  Here's how I pick those notes (which may or may not transpose) that come at the end of the intro to beging the song:  A strum of (G) is going to begin the song, so after you play the (Em) in the intro move to the (G), which will open the 4th string.  Pick that open 4th string <d>, then, still holding the (G) shape, roll flat whichever finger you use to play the 5th string down onto the 4th string and pick the now covered 4th string <e>, then pick the open 3rd string <g> and you're ready to strum (G) on "knew."   

TIP:  When you get to the (Em7) (C) just lift off your finger from the (Em) to open the 4th string.

TIP:  The extra chord at the end of some lines (or before and after a word) means to linger and strum an extra chord measure.

TIP:  The  d u u d u  that appear in the chorus is the strum pattern for that particular section of the song, still holding the (D) and finishing with a single strum of |Em|, and with a slight hesitation between the two upstrums.  Kind of important to the flavor of the song.      


